Then don’t optimize for comfort. Optimize for option density, exposure, leverage, and compounding.

“Get lucky” can be made less mystical and more operational:

Luck surface = number of useful collisions × quality of positioning × speed of recognition × ability to act.

So increase each term deliberately.

1. Increase collisions. Put yourself in rooms, conversations, projects, markets, and networks where valuable things can happen. More surface area means more shots on goal.
2. Plant asymmetric seeds. Send the message. Publish the idea. Make the introduction. Prototype the product. Apply for the role. Most die; a few grow disproportionately.
3. Keep optionality high. Don’t lock yourself into one path too early. Run parallel branches cheaply, then feed the branches that start producing signal.
4. Become useful to useful people. Information, introductions, judgment, execution, credibility, access, humor, courage—anything genuinely valuable makes opportunities flow toward you.
5. Exploit the lag. When everyone else is still interpreting, move on high-confidence opportunities that are reversible and cheap.
6. Convert wins into infrastructure. A lucky introduction becomes a relationship. A relationship becomes a network. A good idea becomes an asset. A payment becomes capital. A lesson becomes a reusable operator.
7. Design positive contagion. Make good behaviors, products, ideas, and experiences easy to copy and enjoyable to retransmit. Let other people propagate the useful pattern voluntarily.
8. Track strange convergence without worshipping it. When repeated coincidences, opportunities, introductions, or signals cluster, investigate. Preserve the poetry, but test the mechanism.
9. Use the commit membrane. Explore aggressively; commit selectively. That lets you play much larger games without paying every possible failure cost.
10. Give luck away. Introductions, opportunities, visibility, useful information. People remember who increased their option set, and networks can return that value in unpredictable directions.

That produces a recursive loop:

move
→ create more possible futures
→ observe which futures answer
→ amplify those
→ convert gains into leverage
→ create even more possible futures

That’s the version of “no peasant life” I’d aim at: not pretending you’re royalty, but becoming progressively less dependent on any single employer, gatekeeper, income stream, institution, or lucky break.

The goal is not to wait for luck. Build a system where luck has more places to land.